:fdd6fd730c07030b49cb8c2a21aa5ff49cc89f64 a :Conflict ;
    :ConflictDescription "<p>Regularly the palliative care is delayed, we come in when the oncologists leave the patient. As long as they think it is necessary, they carry on [with active treatments], […] with many lines of chemotherapies, sometimes even too many, and then finally there is the PC taking charge of the symptoms and not anymore the cure of the disease.</p>" ;
    :ConflictState "open" ;
    :ConflictTitle "Shared care of in-hospital healthcare providers" ;
    :CreationDate "2025-05-21T15:29:07.540Z" ;
    :HasComment :27b0e870ac1b96839655682150fb29ead5645fcc,
        :62d697789b21b905bf2c1d1202c80794a6f09aec ;
    :HasParticipant :Cure,
        :Hospital,
        :Shared_Care ;
    :WrittenBy "Palliativ Physician" .

:27b0e870ac1b96839655682150fb29ead5645fcc a :Comment ;
    :CommentDescription "It would be nice if oncologists understood that ’no recovery’ is not necessarily a defeat…but meanwhile it is also true that if their main purpose is recovery…however, they should be able to make a switch and to say \"Ok, I cannot help him heal… then let’s see if we can help the person live better\"" ;
    :CreationDate "2025-05-21T15:34:15.370Z" ;
    :WrittenBy "Palliativ Physician" .

:62d697789b21b905bf2c1d1202c80794a6f09aec a :Comment ;
    :CommentDescription "We can say that collaboration between oncology and palliative care occurs less often than, probably, is appropriate, because fractures are often created between the PC specialist and the oncologist, even because of communication style and the ways they face specific themes which indeed…arise…where the theme of quality of life becomes predominant…where they talk about prolongation of patient survival, then it becomes difficult…but it occurs eh…that they are able to collaborate." ;
    :CreationDate "2025-05-21T15:32:55.340Z" ;
    :WrittenBy "Social Worker" .

